<p style="color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem;">
By <a href="/en/urban-gavelin/" rel="author">Urban Gavelin</a>, <time datetime="2026-09-01">September 1, 2026</time>, AI Strategy &amp; Adoption
</p>
<h1>Six AI Systems. Not a Single Tool.</h1>
<img src={coverImage} alt="Six AI Systems. Not a Single Tool." width="1280" height="720" class="hero-img" />
<p class="lead"><strong>I have six AI systems running in my office right now. And none of them is really an AI tool.</strong></p>

<div class="section">
<h2>Tools Solve Tasks, Systems Change How You Work</h2>
<p>That might sound like semantics. A bit like when someone points out that a tomato is technically a fruit and ruins the whole salad. But the difference matters. In fact, it is probably the most important thing I have learned about AI in the past year.</p><p>It took me about a year to understand it. Here it is, so maybe you can skip that year: a tool solves a task. A system changes how you work.</p><p>A tool is fairly simple. You find something exciting, buy a license, and one person on the team gets fired up about it. Everyone is supposed to start using it. Three months later, it is roughly the same one person still using it, the one who sends links at 10:43 pm with a message like: 'Have you seen THIS feature?!' Everyone else has quietly gone back to their old ways of working.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>What Is the Difference?</h2>
<p>A system does not live alone. It connects to your data, your workflows, your experience, and everything you have already written, thought, said and learned. When you add a new piece, it is not just that piece that gets better. The other pieces get better too.</p><p>I currently have six such systems running. A personal assistant that sorts incoming messages, prepares materials and keeps track of follow-ups. A second brain where my talks, texts, notes and ideas are searchable. A content system that helps me produce and organise what I publish. Automated research ahead of meetings. Production of training material. And a testing environment where I run language models locally on my own hardware.</p><p>It might sound advanced, but here is the fun part: on its own, none of these systems is particularly impressive. My AI assistant, for example, does things a capable intern could do. And that is completely fine, because the point was never the individual pieces. It is the connections between them. Say I am about to meet a client. The system pulls information about the company and what the person I am meeting has written or said publicly. Plenty of AI services can do that today. But then it goes to my second brain and looks for what I have already said about similar companies, situations and challenges. Suddenly I do not just get 'here is a summary of the client.' I get 'here is the client, measured against my own knowledge and experience.' Almost anyone can get the first kind of information. Only I can get the second, because no AI service in the world has my second brain. And it does not have yours either.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>Build for Yourself First</h2>
<p>Not for the customers. Not for the leadership team. Not for the slide that says 'AI Transformation 2027' next to a picture of a robot shaking hands with a human. Build for your own week. Ask yourself: what do I do over and over that AI could actually help me with?</p><p>Start there and you get two things at once. The time you genuinely save, and a real case. Not a demo, not a 'what if we could...', but: this is how I used to do it, this is how I do it now, this worked, this did not, and this is what I save every single week.</p><p>I try not to talk about AI solutions I do not actually use myself, not to play the strict AI purist, but because otherwise I honestly would not know what I am talking about. You cannot see in a demo what works brilliantly for the first three days and then quietly gets abandoned in week four. You do not know where a system breaks until you have lived with it. And here is the slightly uncomfortable part. If you buy an AI tool for your sales team before you have built and used something yourself, you are buying something you cannot really evaluate. You do not know what questions to ask the vendor, what is reasonable to expect, or whether the real problem is the technology, the process, or the people. When the team then does not use the tool, the easy conclusion is: 'the tool did not work.' Except the problem might have been something else entirely. Order matters. First your own week, then the team's. First experience, then scale.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>So What Do You Do on Monday?</h2>
<p>Pick one single thing you do every week that does not really require you specifically. Something administrative, recurring, and preferably a bit dull. You probably already know what it is, the thing you do while thinking, 'why am I still the one doing this?'</p><p>Then write down exactly how you do it today, step by step. It sounds absurdly simple, but that description is often half the work, and most people skip it. If you cannot describe the process yourself, how is an AI supposed to understand it?</p><p>Do not build the best possible solution. Build the simplest solution that works, and run it for two weeks. Not two hours, two weeks. Only then do you see what actually happens when the technology meets reality. During those two weeks, track exactly one thing: what am I no longer doing by hand? Not how impressive the system looks, not how many AI agents you have running or how many integrations are blinking green on your screen. What do you actually get to stop doing? That is the point where AI stops being interesting technology and starts actually changing your working day. So I will leave you with a question: which of your systems would get better if it already knew what you know? Think about it. The answer might well be the start of your next AI investment.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<div itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
<h3 itemprop="name">What is the real difference between an AI tool and an AI system?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<p itemprop="text">An AI tool solves a single task on its own, while a system connects to your data, workflows and past experience so that every new part makes the other parts better too. That is why systems change how you work overall, not just what you can accomplish in one isolated moment.</p>
</div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
<h3 itemprop="name">Where should I start if I want to build my own AI system?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<p itemprop="text">Start with your own week, not your team's and not your customers'. Pick one recurring task that does not really require your personal judgement, write down exactly how you do it today, then build the simplest possible solution and run it for a full two weeks before you judge the result.</p>
</div>
</div>

